# Graduate Micro Theory
#
# The first-year doctoral core as a theorem ledger: each result beside the hypotheses it needs, so a reader can see what efficiency proves and what it leaves open.

#>economics_grad_micro
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!economics
#:form #!ledger
#:level #!graduate
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bane

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Graduate Micro Theory"
 ~#claim: "The welfare theorems hold only under stated hypotheses, and a Pareto efficient allocation can still be extremely unequal; mechanism design shows what private information costs."
 ~#proof: "Entries cite Arrow, Debreu, Kakutani, Pratt, Akerlof, Spence, Gibbard, Satterthwaite, Myerson, Vickrey, Clarke, and Groves, with Mas-Colell, Whinston, and Green as the standard text."
 ~#door: "Read a theorem with its hypotheses beside it, draw the Edgeworth box, then test one hypothesis against a market you know."
 ~#audience: "first-year graduate students and readers of the welfare theorems"
 continuity: "Graduate Micro Theory | hypotheses beside"
 cold_open: "Keep the hypotheses beside every theorem, and you will see that an efficient allocation can still leave one person with almost nothing."
}

